{"task_id": "HumanEval/26", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict\n\n\ndef _count_occurrences(numbers: List[int]) -> Dict[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"Counts the frequency of each element in the input list.\n    It returns a dictionary mapping each number to its occurrence count.\n    \"\"\"\n    counts: Dict[int, int] = {}\n    for num in numbers:\n        counts[num] = counts.get(num, 0) + 1\n    return counts\n\n\ndef _filter_unique_elements(numbers: List[int], counts: Dict[int, int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Identifies elements appearing exactly once and preserves their original relative order.\n    It iterates through the input list, appending elements with a count of 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    filtered_elements: List[int] = []\n    for num in numbers:\n        if counts[num] == 1:\n            filtered_elements.append(num)\n    return filtered_elements\n\n\ndef _preserve_order(original: List[int], filtered: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"Ensures the order of truly unique elements is maintained as in the original input.\n    As _filter_unique_elements already produces an ordered list, this function returns it directly.\n    \"\"\"\n    return filtered\n\n\ndef remove_duplicates(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" From a list of integers, remove all elements that occur more than once.\n    Keep order of elements left the same as in the input.\n    >>> remove_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 2, 4])\n    [1, 3, 4]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    counts = _count_occurrences(numbers)\n    uniques_in_order = _filter_unique_elements(numbers, counts)\n    final_list = _preserve_order(numbers, uniques_in_order)\n    return final_list\n```"}
