{"task_id": "HumanEval/96", "solution": "def validate_non_negative_integer(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if the input integer is non-negative.\n    This function assumes 'n' is already an integer based on critical requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    return n >= 0\n\ndef is_prime(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Determines if a given number is prime using trial division.\n    It handles base cases for numbers less than 2 and even numbers efficiently.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 2:\n        return False\n    if number == 2:\n        return True\n    if number % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    i = 3\n    while i * i <= number:\n        if number % i == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 2\n    return True\n\ndef generate_primes_up_to(n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Generates an unsorted list of prime numbers less than n.\n    It iterates from 2 up to n-1 and applies the primality test.\n    \"\"\"\n    primes = []\n    for num in range(2, n):\n        if is_prime(num):\n            primes.append(num)\n    return primes\n\ndef format_prime_list(primes: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Sorts a list of prime numbers in ascending order.\n    It returns a new sorted list based on the input.\n    \"\"\"\n    return sorted(primes)\n\ndef count_up_to(n: int) -> list:\n    \"\"\"Implements a function that takes a non-negative integer and returns an array of\n    prime numbers less than the input value in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not validate_non_negative_integer(n):\n        # This branch would ideally raise an error, but critical requirements forbid it.\n        # Given critical requirements, 'n' is trusted to be a non-negative integer.\n        # Thus, validate_non_negative_integer(n) will always be True in practice.\n        pass\n\n    if n <= 1:\n        return []\n\n    unsorted_primes = generate_primes_up_to(n)\n    sorted_primes = format_prime_list(unsorted_primes)\n    return sorted_primes"}
